// A first-order problem with no Java in it, used by the SMT degradation test.
//
// It has to be something an SMT solver can actually be handed: a proof obligation from a Java
// method contract still contains modalities at its root, so the translation would fail before a
// solver was ever launched, and the test would then be about the translator rather than about a
// missing solver binary. This is valid and trivially translatable, so a working solver answers
// "valid" and only a broken one does not.
\problem {
    \forall int x; \forall int y; (x <= y | y <= x)
}
